questions.question_text:
The network diagram shows the road distances, in kilometres, connecting five towns $A, B, C, D, E$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.4);
\coordinate (B) at (2.2,2.6);
\coordinate (C) at (2.2,0.2);
\coordinate (D) at (4.4,1.4);
\coordinate (E) at (6.4,1.4);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, above left] {$11$};
\draw (A) -- (C) node[midway, below left] {$14$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$8$};
\draw (B) -- (D) node[midway, above right] {$16$};
\draw (C) -- (D) node[midway, below right] {$9$};
\draw (D) -- (E) node[midway, above] {$18$};
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
What is the total length of the path $A\text{–}C\text{–}D\text{–}E$?

questions.solution_text:
1. **Trace the sequence of vertices along valid edges**
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.4);
\coordinate (B) at (2.2,2.6);
\coordinate (C) at (2.2,0.2);
\coordinate (D) at (4.4,1.4);
\coordinate (E) at (6.4,1.4);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, above left] {$11$};
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (A) -- (C) node[midway, below left] {$14$};
\draw (B) -- (C) node[midway, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$8$};
\draw (B) -- (D) node[midway, above right] {$16$};
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (C) -- (D) node[midway, below right] {$9$};
\draw[line width=1.6pt] (D) -- (E) node[midway, above] {$18$};
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
2. **Sum the weights of the traversed edges**
$\text{Total length} = 14 + 9 + 18$
$= 41\text{ km}$

questions.question_text:
Two bicycle paths connect Home to School:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,1.4);
\coordinate (B) at (2.5,2.6);
\coordinate (C) at (2.5,0.2);
\coordinate (D) at (5,1.4);
\draw (A) -- (B) node[midway, above left] {$8$};
\draw (B) -- (D) node[midway, above right] {$11$};
\draw (A) -- (C) node[midway, below left] {$6$};
\draw (C) -- (D) node[midway, below right] {$10$};
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
How much time is saved by taking Path 2 ($A\text{–}C\text{–}D$) rather than Path 1 ($A\text{–}B\text{–}D$)?

A network path $P\text{–}Q\text{–}R\text{–}S$ has a total length of $38\text{ km}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (P) at (0,1.2);
\coordinate (Q) at (2.2,2.4);
\coordinate (R) at (4.4,0.6);
\coordinate (S) at (6.6,1.8);
\draw (P) -- (Q) node[midway, above left] {$11$};
\draw (Q) -- (R) node[midway, above right] {$x$};
\draw (R) -- (S) node[midway, above left] {$14$};
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$P$};
\fill (Q) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$Q$};
\fill (R) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$R$};
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
What is the value of $x$?

